<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theuderic I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 485–<a href="/years/533">533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theuderic I was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, who received the eastern part of his father's realm in 511 and ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> until his death in 533 or 534. His kingdom became the core of Austrasia. With his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> he conquered the Kingdom of the Thuringians around 531, and he also campaigned in Auvergne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theuderic-i.jpg" alt="Theuderic I" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_de_bronze_de_Thierry_Ier.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Theuderic was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> by one of his earlier wives or concubines, possibly a Franco-Rhenish princess named Evochildis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. In accordance with Salian tradition, Clovis's kingdom was divided at his death in 511 between his four surviving sons. Theuderic inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Chlodomer Orléans and Chlothar I Soissons. Theuderic's realm is called the kingdom of Metz, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a> or of Austrasia, according to the writer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign Theuderic sent his son Theudebert to kill the Scandinavian king Chlochilaich, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hygelac">Hygelac</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a>, who had invaded his realm. He then became involved in the war between the Thuringian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herminafrid">Hermanfrid</a> and his brother Baderic, and was promised half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> for his help. Baderic was defeated, but the land promised was not handed over. In 531 Theuderic, with his brother Chlothar and his son, attacked Thuringia to avenge himself.</p><p>With the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> under Duke Hadugato, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> was conquered. Chlothar received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegund">Radegund</a>, daughter of King Berthar, who had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herminafrid">Hermanfrid</a>'s brother. Hermanfrid was killed in battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unstrut">Unstrut</a> and his kingdom was annexed. The four sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> then fought the Burgundian kings Sigismund and Godomar. Godomar fled and Sigismund was taken prisoner by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodomer">Chlodomer</a>, while Theuderic married Sigismund's daughter Suavegotha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Godomar rallied the Burgundian army and won back his kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlodomer">Chlodomer</a>, aided by Theuderic, defeated Godomar again but died in the fighting at Vézeronce. After making a treaty with his brother Childebert, Theuderic died, and the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> passed without hindrance, and unexpectedly, to his son Theudebert. His daughter Theodechild, by Suavegotha, founded the Abbey of St-Pierre le Vif at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theuderic I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Austrasia, the eastern land, arose from the share of Clovis' eldest son <b>Theuderic I</b> in 511, ruled from Reims and later Metz.</blockquote><a href="/state/austrasia">Austrasia</a></li><li><blockquote>After Theodoric's death removed Ostrogothic protection, the Frankish kings <b>Theuderic I</b> and Chlothar I defeated the Thuringians on the Unstrut in 531; Herminafrid was killed soon after at Zülpich.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-thuringians">Kingdom of the Thuringians</a></li></ul></section>
